{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01X1vs7mRGUrzpVsCB8C7Gr4","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Cursor-cell numbering by discovery order (companion to the row carrier)\n2\t\n3\tStatus: **Adopted by the owner 2026-10-12 (replaces carrier §6's\n4\trefinement and search); not yet implemented.** Round 4 of the same day\n5\t(Architect, claude-fable-5-1): the base design the owner asked for after\n6\tthe third review (`collab/1792210000000-…review-r3…findings.md`), with\n7\tthe round-3 variants moved to appendix B as an extension that is not\n8\tadopted. Subordinate to\n9\t[`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md) (the\n10\tcarrier, body version 3) and\n11\t[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md);\n12\tvocabulary is UCF §7.5.3 (\"Terminology: cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref,\n13\tcell\") exactly. Evidence is slice 6a's `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc`\n14\tand `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc` (branch `srvm-phase6a` at\n15\t`d77da9f0`) and the prototype of appendix A. §§1–6 are normative; the\n16\tappendices are not.\n17\t\n18\t**The rule in one paragraph.** A cursor cell is named by the order in\n19\twhich a fixed walk over the task meets it: the n-th cursor cell met is\n20\t`:yin.k/cc-n`, as a de Bruijn index names a binder by position. The walk\n21\tis pre-order in the carrier's table order, so every position is ordered\n22\texcept the members of a guest set and the entries of a guest map. Those\n23\tthe carrier *transforms* into ordered vectors before numbering: each\n24\tmember is given a key, the spelling of the member in which cursor cells\n25\talready numbered appear by number and the others by body, occurrence\n26\tcount and the order in which they first appear inside the member; the\n27\tmembers are sorted by key. Members whose keys are equal are *tied*; they\n28\tare postponed until the rest of the walk has numbered what it can, and\n29\tre-keyed. A tie that survives is entered in any order if a cheap test\n30\tproves its members interchangeable (swapping them leaves the whole state\n31\tunchanged); otherwise the lift refuses. Guest programs are unrestricted:\n32\tsets and maps may hold cursor-refs anywhere, including as map keys.\n33\t\n34\t**Example.** `[P Q P]` numbers `P = cc-1`, `Q = cc-2` whatever the bodies.\n35\tIn `{'a #{X Y}, 'b X}` with equal bodies, the set is reached first; `X`\n36\toccurs twice in the task and `Y` once, so their keys differ and `Y` is\n37\tentered first: `Y = cc-1`, `X = cc-2` (the root is 6a's, `2d9873a8…`).\n38\tIn `#{[A B] [B A]}` the two pairs have equal keys and share both cells;\n39\tthe test cannot prove them interchangeable (they are) and the lift\n40\trefuses `:unresolved-cursor-cells`. In `#{[A B] [B C]}` the keys differ\n41\t(`B` occurs twice) and the path numbers `A, B, C = cc-1, cc-2, cc-3`.\n42\t\n43\t## 1. The rule\n44\t\n45\t**Vocabulary.** An **item** is a row or a value. The **walk** is the\n46\tprocedure of §1.2 over one item from a numbering; the **root walk** is\n47\tover the `:task` row from the empty numbering and its numbers are the\n48\toutput; a **key walk** is the same procedure over one member of a set or\n49\tmap from the enclosing walk's numbering, run only to produce the member's\n50\t**key**. A **member** is a `:set` element or a `:map` entry taken as the\n51\tsynthetic ordered row `[:entry key value]`. A **tie group** is a run of\n52\tmembers of one container with equal keys. A cursor cell's **body rank**\n53\tis the index of its census body label `[:body [:stream identity\n54\tdescriptor] position]` among the census's distinct labels in bytewise\n55\torder; its **occurrence count** is the number of positions of the task\n56\t(census and install children excluded) that hold it. Install children\n57\tare numbered as their own tasks by the same rule, in any order.\n58\t\n59\t### 1.1 The transformation and the key\n60\t\n61\tWhen the walk reaches a `:set` or `:map` it replaces the container, for\n62\tits own purposes, by the vector of its members in key order. The key of\n63\ta member is the **spelling** of that member produced by a key walk under\n64\tthe numbering at that moment (every member of one container is keyed\n65\tfrom that one numbering):\n66\t\n67\t- a cursor cell numbered by the enclosing walk spells `[:cursor-cell\n68\t  [:n k]]` (root numbers) or as the enclosing key walk spelled it;\n69\t- a cursor cell the key walk meets unnumbered receives a **local index**\n70\t  `j` (the order of first meeting, counted from the enclosing walk's\n71\t  local counter so a nested key walk never reuses an index of its\n72\t  enclosing one) and spells `[:cursor-cell [:u j r m]]`, `r` its body\n73\t  rank and `m` its occurrence count, all exact CBOR integers;\n74\t- `[:lit d]` as itself, structural data as itself (by reference, never\n75\t  copied), `[:row B]` expanded in place, the census as `[:cursor-cells]`,\n76\t  an install child as `[:child]`, keyed structural rows in canonical key\n77\t  order, `:window` in register order, and a nested `:set` or `:map` as\n78\t  the bytewise sorted vector of its members' spellings in which a tie\n79\t  group the key walk could not certify (§1.3) is spelled in place as\n80\t  `[:tie K … K]`, its common key once per member.\n81\t\n82\tKeys are compared bytewise on canonical CBOR; the spelling is hash-free.\n83\t`[X X]` keys `[:vec [u0 r m] [u0 r m]]` and `[Y Z]` keys\n84\t`[:vec [u0 r m] [u1 r m']]`: the alias pattern inside a member is part\n85\tof its key, and so is each cell's occurrence count, which separates\n86\t`#{[A B] [B C]}` and `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` without a search (appendix A).\n87\t\n88\t### 1.2 The walk\n89\t\n90\t**W1, order.** Depth-first, pre-order, from the walk's item. A row's\n91\tslots in table order (`rows/row-table`, `rows.cljc:37-57`); ordered\n92\trepeated slots (`frames`, `:vec`, `:list`, `:k`) in order; keyed rows\n93\twith structural keys (`:bindings`, `:store`, `:parked-table`, the root's\n94\t`installs` and `module-stores`) in the Jing canonical byte order of the\n95\tkey; `:window` in ascending register order. The census is not entered;\n96\tan install child's `:task` row is not entered. A `:set` or `:map` is\n97\tentered as §1.1 orders it: members with a unique key in key order, an\n98\tentry's key before its value; each tie group is **postponed** to the\n99\twalk's queue (queue order is canonical: containers in walk order, groups\n100\twithin one container in key order).\n101\t\n102\t**W2, meeting.** When the walk reaches a `[:cursor-cell C]` value or a\n103\t`C` slot of a `:pending` row whose cursor cell has no number, the cell\n104\treceives the next number (the root walk counts from 1). A numbered cell\n105\tis passed over. Every occurrence emits `[:cursor-cell :yin.k/cc-n]`; the\n106\tcensus row stays the contents table.\n107\t\n108\t**W3, postponed ties.** After the pre-order walk, the queue is taken in\n109\torder: each group's members are re-keyed under the current numbering and\n110\ttreated as in W1 (unique keys entered in key order, surviving ties\n111\tre-queued in order; a tie met inside an entered member joins the same\n112\tqueue). Repeat while a pass numbers at least one cursor cell and groups\n113\tremain. When a pass numbers nothing, the surviving groups go to the tie\n114\ttest.\n115\t\n116\t### 1.3 The tie test\n117\t\n118\tA surviving group **passes** when all of (i) its common key is complete\n119\t(contains no `[:tie …]`), (ii) the unnumbered cursor cells of its members\n120\tare pairwise disjoint, and (iii) none of them occurs in a member of any\n121\tother surviving group of the same walk. (At this point every unnumbered\n122\tcell occurs only in postponed members, so (ii) and (iii) say: the cells\n123\tof each member are confined to that member.) If every surviving group\n124\tpasses, all of them are entered, each group's members in the order the\n125\thost lists them (§2 shows the rows do not depend on it), and the walk\n126\treturns to W3. If any group fails, the **root walk refuses** (§1.4) and a\n127\t**key walk stops**: its surviving groups are spelled as `[:tie …]`\n128\tresidue and its key is incomplete. The test inside a key walk reads only\n129\tthe member being keyed; it never reads the enclosing state.\n130\t\n131\tThis is the cheapest sound test: (i)–(iii) are what is needed for the\n132\tindex-matched swap of two members to be an automorphism of the state;\n133\twhatever it refuses may still be interchangeable (§3 says what), but\n134\tnothing it accepts can be host-order dependent.\n135\t\n136\t### 1.4 Refusal, and decode\n137\t\n138\tThe root walk refuses `:yin.k/non-portable` kind\n139\t`:unresolved-cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/path` the row path of the container\n140\tholding the first failing group in queue order (a container nested under\n141\ta member of a set or map has the enclosing container's path followed by\n142\t`:member`, since no index into an unordered container is canonical; for\n143\tan install child the path is prefixed `[12 <install name> …]`), and\n144\t`:yin.k/cursor-cells` the sorted unnumbered cursor cells of the failing\n145\tgroups' members. The verdict and the path are functions of the state up\n146\tto renaming and host enumeration; the cell list names local source ids.\n147\tThis is not a resource refusal and never `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`.\n148\t\n149\tThe receiver decodes, recomputes the numbering of the root and of every\n150\tinstall child, re-emits and compares root ids\n151\t(`:yin.k/undecodable :noncanonical-cursor-cells` on mismatch). In 6a's\n152\t`decode*` the recomputation is the `emit` call (`rows.cljc:570-575`);\n153\tthe **whole** of that call is wrapped so that only a lift-side\n154\t`:yin.k/non-portable :unresolved-cursor-cells` (root or child) becomes\n155\t`:yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells` with `:yin.k/path` prefixed\n156\tby the carried root id (a child's path reads `[<root> 12 <name> …]`); a\n157\tresource refusal propagates as itself; nothing else can arise there,\n158\tsince step 5 has checked literals and markers. The validator order stays:\n159\tnumbering (step 6) after step 5 and before `validate-frames`\n160\t(`rows.cljc:576-578`; test `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`).\n161\t\n162\t### 1.5 Budgets and gates\n163\t\n164\t- `:cursor-cells`: the census size is charged first, before ranks,\n165\t  counts or any walk (6a's gate at `rows.cljc:256`, kept).\n166\t- `:depth`: the walk's nesting; a member step counts one level (6a's\n167\t  gate at `rows.cljc:291-292`, kept, now in the walk).\n168\t- `:work`: one unit per position visited (walk, key walk, cell scans,\n169\t  count and rank preparation) or spelled, one unit per cell in a key\n170\t  walk's memo key, and, **before every CBOR encoding made for a\n171\t  comparison** (keys, rank labels, structural keys), the representation\n172\t  bound of the value encoded, computed by reading it: 6a's `size` of\n173\t  `check-label-bytes!` (`rows.cljc:218-229`), an upper bound of the\n174\t  encoding's bytes. Spellings hold input literals by reference, so their\n175\t  own size is their spelled positions. Hence every byte allocated for a\n176\t  comparison is charged before the allocation, and the bytes alive at\n177\t  any time are bounded by the budget plus the input. No polynomial bound\n178\t  is claimed; the budget is the bound. Default `4194304`, the scale of\n179\t  the deleted `:label-bytes` (appendix A: at most 624926 over 3300\n180\t  generated states, 544554 with a 65536-character stream descriptor). A\n181\t  literal of more than about 30000 scalars inside a guest set or map\n182\t  exceeds it (6a allowed about 8000); in an ordered position a literal is\n183\t  never spelled and costs one unit.\n184\t- `:rows`, `:bytes`: unchanged. `:label-bytes` and `:search` are deleted.\n185\t\n186\tExceeding any budget is the resource refusal of carrier §10.10\n187\t(`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [<key>]`), never a canonicality\n188\tverdict, at lift and at decode alike.\n189\t\n190\t### 1.6 Totality\n191\t\n192\tThe root walk ends with an empty queue or a refusal. Before emission the\n193\timplementation asserts that the numbering is a bijection from the\n194\tcensus's cursor cells onto `1 … n`; a violation (a census cell no walk\n195\treaches) is a defect, an exception without `:yin.k/status` carrying the\n196\tmissing and extra cells, never a refusal and never a partially numbered\n197\tcarrier. 6a's test `input-row-bodies-do-not-spend-label-bytes`\n198\t(`rows_test.cljc:406-421`) declares exactly such a cell in `base` and\n199\texpects `emit` to succeed (`:414`); §4 rewrites it.\n200\t\n201\t## 2. Proof of canonicality\n202\t\n203\tCall two configurations (abstract task, numbering, queue) **isomorphic**\n204\twhen a bijection `π` of cursor cells carries one to the other up to the\n205\thost order of every set, map and census, with `N'(π(c)) = N(c)` and\n206\tqueues corresponding group by group. **Theorem:** on isomorphic\n207\tconfigurations the procedure refuses at corresponding points or emits\n208\tbyte-identical rows. The proof is an induction on the measure\n209\t`(size of the walk's item as a sub-term, unnumbered cells of the walk)`.\n210\t\n211\t*Sort.* The key of a member is a function of the member and the\n212\tnumbering up to isomorphism. The key walk reads structure, numbers,\n213\tranks (a function of the census up to isomorphism) and occurrence counts\n214\t(an isomorphism invariant); it orders nested containers by the keys of\n215\ttheir members, canonical by the induction (a member is a proper sub-term,\n216\tso the measure decreases); its only other listed-order input is the\n217\torder in which it enters an interchangeable group, harmless by the tie\n218\tstep below; and what it cannot certify it spells as `[:tie K … K]`, a\n219\tfunction of `K` and the group's size. Equal keys therefore mean\n220\tisomorphic members when the key is complete (a complete spelling is\n221\tfaithful: structure slot by slot, every cell by local index, rank and\n222\tcount, so matching indices is a bijection of the members' cells under\n223\twhich the members agree).\n224\t\n225\t*Walk.* W1 and W2 read only table order and keys; W3 repeats W1 on the\n226\tqueue in a canonical order. So the numbering before the tie test, the\n227\tsurviving groups and their keys are functions of the configuration up to\n228\tisomorphism, and so is the verdict of the test, whose inputs (key\n229\tcompleteness, the sets of unnumbered cells of members) are structural.\n230\t\n231\t*Tie step.* Let `G` pass with members `e_1 … e_k`. Their keys are equal\n232\tand complete, so for each pair there is an index-matched bijection\n233\t`π_ij` of their unnumbered cells carrying `e_i` to `e_j`; the cells of\n234\tdistinct members are disjoint (ii) and occur nowhere else in the state\n235\t(iii), so the permutation composed from these bijections, extended by\n236\tthe identity, is an automorphism of the whole state that permutes the\n237\tmembers of `G` and fixes everything else. Entering `G` in two host orders\n238\t`σ`, `σ'` gives configurations related by that automorphism: numbers\n239\tagree by construction (the cells of the t-th entered member receive the\n240\tsame block, by local index), structures agree, queues agree. The rest of\n241\tthe procedure is equivariant by the theorem's induction, so the rows are\n242\tthe same. Passing groups are mutually independent by (iii), so entering\n243\tall of them before the next pass changes nothing.\n244\t\n245\t*Emission.* Cells are emitted by number only and every unordered slot is\n246\tsorted by canonical bytes under the final numbering (carrier §5), so\n247\tisomorphic final configurations emit the same rows. ∎\n248\t\n249\t*Termination.* There is no search and no re-entry: the only recursion is\n250\tthe key walk, over a proper sub-term of its caller's item, so key walks\n251\tnest at most as deep as the structure (`:depth`). Within one walk, every\n252\tW3 pass except the last numbers a cell, and every tie step either numbers\n253\ta cell or replaces the queued members by proper sub-terms of themselves\n254\t(members with no unnumbered cell are equal values, which the carrier\n255\trefuses as duplicates at §8 step 5). The measure decreases at each step\n256\tand the budget bounds the count.\n257\t\n258\tConsequences: a function of structure, invariant under renaming and host\n259\tenumeration (the theorem with `π` a renaming, a permutation of\n260\tcontainers, or both); deterministic, so two lifts of one state are\n261\tbyte-identical and the receiver's recomputation is well defined; sharing\n262\tsurvives (one number per cell, every occurrence spells it); the\n263\tcross-engine law of continuation §2.1 follows as before.\n264\t\n265\t## 3. What is refused\n266\t\n267\tRefused iff, when the root walk's last pass numbers nothing, some\n268\tsurviving tie group fails §1.3. Concretely one of:\n269\t\n270\t- **(i) an incomplete key**: a tied member contains a container whose\n271\t  own ties the key walk could not certify. `#{#{[A B] [B A]} #{[C D]\n272\t  [D C]}}` (interchangeable; refused because equal residue keys need not\n273\t  mean isomorphic members: `#{[A B] [B A]}` and `#{[A B] [C D]}` key\n274\t  alike).\n275\t- **(ii) two tied members share a cell**: the 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}`,\n276\t  the 3-cycle, the loop plus 2-cycle `#{[L L] [A B] [B A]}`\n277\t  (`rows_test.cljc:312-329`, where `L` is separated by its key, `A B`\n278\t  are not), the mixed-body `#{[A B] [A C]}` with `A` on another stream\n279\t  position than `B, C` (all interchangeable and refused), and the\n280\t  seven-cell triangle plus square (`rows_test.cljc:392-397`), which is\n281\t  **not** interchangeable: a triangle edge entered first and a square\n282\t  edge entered first give different rows, so no sound test can accept it\n283\t  without a search.\n284\t- **(iii) a tied member's cell occurs in a tied member of another\n285\t  container**: `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, `[#{#{X Y}} #{[X Z] [Y W]}]`, and the\n286\t  reviewer's anchored state `[#{[A B] [A C] [B D] [C E] [D B] [E C]} A]`\n287\t  (`A` is numbered by its ordered position, which does not break the tie\n288\t  of the rest; all three interchangeable and refused).\n289\t\n290\tEqual bodies are not required (the mixed-body case) and an ordered anchor\n291\tdoes not certify a whole component (the anchored case). 6a numbers the\n292\t2-cycle, the 3-cycle, the loop plus 2-cycle, `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, the\n293\tmixed and the anchored states, and refuses the seven-cell state and the\n294\ttwo-2-cycle state at `[:label-bytes]`. Discovery order numbers what 6a\n295\trefuses at `[:label-bytes]` when the keys separate the members, and\n296\tnumbers the path and `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]`, which 6a also numbers.\n297\t\n298\tMeasured (appendix A; refusals classified by entering the ties in every\n299\tlisted order of every container when there are at most 720 such orders,\n300\telse 48 sampled orders):\n301\t\n302\t| Generator | States | Numbered | Refused | of which interchangeable | not interchangeable | undecided |\n303\t|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n304\t| random values (1–6 cells, two bodies, nested sets, maps with cursor-ref keys, edge sets, sets of sets) | 2000 | 1981 | 19 | 16 | 3 | 0 |\n305\t| frame-bearing (windows, K frames, environments, parked state) | 200 | 196 | 4 | 3 | 1 | 0 |\n306\t| with install children | 100 | 98 | 2 | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n307\t| reviewer's tie-heavy incidence families | 1000 | 832 | 168 | 92 | 17 | 59 |\n308\t\n309\tEvery refusal was (ii) or (iii); none was (i). Invariance failures and\n310\tdecode failures: 0 of 3300. No production corpus exists yet (no\n311\tversion-3 lift before 6b), so these are generator rates, not frequencies.\n312\t\n313\t**Fixtures.** Every state named in this section and in §1's example is a\n314\tfixture of §5 (items 12–16), including the refused ones.\n315\t\n316\t## 4. Replacing 6a: deleted, rewritten, kept\n317\t\n318\tAgainst `srvm-phase6a` (`d77da9f0`), `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` and\n319\t`test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc` (line numbers of those files).\n320\t\n321\t**rows.cljc, deleted.** `check-label-bytes!` (`:212-230`) as a bound on\n322\tlabels (its `size` function, `:218-229`, survives as the representation\n323\tbound of §1.5); inside `canonical-numbering` (`:236-355`): the label\n324\tseeding (`:252-255`), `ce-slot`/`ce` (`:257-284`), `positions`\n325\t(`:285-325`) except its depth gate, `classes` (`:326-330`), `refine`\n326\t(`:331-337`), `search` (`:338-354`), the `checked-*` wrappers\n327\t(`:245-251`); in `default-budget` (`:200-202`) the keys `:label-bytes`\n328\tand `:search`.\n329\t\n330\t**rows.cljc, rewritten.**\n331\t- `canonical-numbering` (`:236-355`), same signature and contract\n332\t  (`task`, optional `budget` → `{source-id :yin.k/cc-n}`; empty census\n333\t  → `{}`): the census gate (`:256`) first; body ranks and occurrence\n334\t  counts; then the root walk of §1.2 with its queue, the tie test, the\n335\t  refusal and the totality assertion, as in the prototype's sections\n336\t  \"cell scans\", \"runs\", \"spelling\" and \"procedure\"\n337\t  (`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj:79-416`), charging as\n338\t  §1.5 says. Portable `.cljc` per continuation §12: no `@#'` reflection;\n339\t  `mapv`/`into`/`reduce`, not `for`, over sequences that may exceed 32\n340\t  elements (the prototype's `group-failure` uses `for`: replace);\n341\t  `(assoc {} …)` never `(assoc nil …)`; atoms are fine on all three\n342\t  hosts. 6a's `bytes-key` (`:91-94`) takes encoded bytes; the prototype's\n343\t  takes a value: keep 6a's and encode first.\n344\t- `descriptors` (`:117-124`): reused, with the synthetic `:entry` row\n345\t  added (`[:V :V]`).\n346\t- `default-budget` (`:200-202`): `:work` default `4194304`.\n347\t- `emit-node` (`:181-197`): thread a row path so a child's refusal\n348\t  reports `[12 <install name> …]`.\n349\t- `decode*` recompute (`:570-575`): the translation of §1.4 around the\n350\t  whole `emit` call.\n351\t- Namespace docstring (`:1-17`): \"canonical cursor-cell numbering\" →\n352\t  \"discovery-order cursor-cell numbering (design note §1)\".\n353\t\n354\t**rows.cljc, kept unchanged.** `row-table`, `pending-table`, `node`,\n355\t`refuse!`/`bad!`, the marker checks, `bytes-key`/`compare-bytes`/\n356\t`byte-compare`/`sorted-bytes`, the census order (`:137-156`),\n357\t`emit-slot`/`emit-node` apart from the path, `spend!` (`:205-209`),\n358\t`emit-numbered` (`:358-363`), `emit` (`:366-372`), `structural?`,\n359\t`literal?`, `check-abstract!`, all of `decode*` before `:570` and after\n360\t`:575`, `decode`, `canonical-under`, `frame->rows`, `rows->frame`,\n361\t`validate-frames`, and the `:bytes`, `:rows`, `:depth`, `:cursor-cells`\n362\tkeys with their defaults.\n363\t\n364\t**rows_test.cljc, kept.** `task-node`, `cursor-cell-body`, `fixture`\n365\t(`:16-37`), `portable-frame` and `bijection`, `row-ids-are-jing-default`,\n366\t`replace-row`, `window-sorted-and-unique`,\n367\t`canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant` (`:143-156`),\n368\t`alias-topology-and-census` (`:159-168`), `gates-and-graph-before-hashes`,\n369\t`numbering-precedes-frame-defect` (`:206-213`),\n370\t`schema-and-literal-canonicality`, `child-scope-and-shared-child-dag`\n371\t(`:240-249`), `pending-envelope-and-nil-literal-are-preserved`,\n372\t`task-root-checks-carried-code-and-frame`, `reversed` and\n373\t`unordered-containers-are-order-free` (`:285-304`),\n374\t`census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells` (`:331-338`), the marker tests\n375\t(`:341-381`), `cursor-cell-and-identity-shaped-literal-are-distinct`\n376\t(`:384-389`), the fixture `seven-cursor-cell-triangle-square`\n377\t(`:392-397`).\n378\t\n379\t**rows_test.cljc, rewritten.**\n380\t- `refinement-search-on-symmetric-cursor-cells-is-bounded` (`:171-178`)\n381\t  → `symmetric-cursor-cells-are-interchangeable`: the two assertions on\n382\t  equal roots and two numbers stay; the `{:search 1}` assertions become\n383\t  `{:work 1}` → `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [:work]`.\n384\t- `canonical-roots-are-pinned` (`:307-328`): `fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`\n385\t  stay; the loop plus 2-cycle block (`:312-328`) asserts the\n386\t  `:unresolved-cursor-cells` refusal with path `[15]` and cells `[:a :b]`\n387\t  for the task and the same verdict and path for `renamed`; the\n388\t  `{:search 2}` assertion and the pin `55ff168e…` go.\n389\t- `intermediate-label-bytes-are-bounded` (`:400-403`) →\n390\t  `seven-cursor-cells-are-refused`: `:yin.k/non-portable\n391\t  :unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[15]`, cells `[:a … :g]`, and the\n392\t  renamed and shuffled state gives the same verdict and path.\n393\t- `input-row-bodies-do-not-spend-label-bytes` (`:406-421`) →\n394\t  `large-literals-in-ordered-positions-are-free`: `base` (its census\n395\t  cell `:x` is unreachable) now asserts the totality defect of §1.6 (an\n396\t  exception whose data is `{:missing [:x] :extra []}` and has no\n397\t  `:yin.k/status`) in place of `(is (nil? (error #(rows/emit base))))`\n398\t  (`:414`); `reachable` and `custody` keep emitting and decoding to the\n399\t  expected tasks (`:415-421`); add: the same 400000-element literal\n400\t  inside a guest set refuses `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit\n401\t  [:work]` under the default budget.\n402\t\n403\t## 5. Acceptance for the rewritten 6a numbering\n404\t\n405\t12. `rows-test/discovery-order-numbering`: `canonical-numbering` returns\n406\t    the maps of appendix A for `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y`, the review\n407\t    counterexample, `[x x y]`, `[x y y]`, `[#{X Y} X]`, the witness\n408\t    `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`, `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]`, the path and the two\n409\t    frame fixtures.\n410\t13. `rows-test/occurrence-count-separates-members`: the review\n411\t    counterexample numbers `Y cc-1, X cc-2`, pinned `2d9873a8…` (6a's\n412\t    root); the path pinned `726a9fbb…`; `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` pinned\n413\t    `d84480d4…`; each equal after renaming plus reversal of every set.\n414\t14. `rows-test/interchangeable-ties`: `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}`,\n415\t    `{X 1, Y 1}`, `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` number without refusal; reversal and\n416\t    renaming give equal roots; pins `07eda35d…`, `da69c679…`,\n417\t    `49d4478c…`, `18b70de6…`.\n418\t15. `rows-test/alias-pattern-is-part-of-the-key`: `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` and\n419\t    `{X X, Y Z}` give one root for both enumerations and after renaming,\n420\t    pinned `387f294a…` and `be62ba43…` (6a's roots); the witness numbers\n421\t    `B cc-1, A cc-2`, pinned `0193312…` (6a's root), and decodes.\n422\t16. `rows-test/unresolved-cursor-cells-refuse`: the 2-cycle, the\n423\t    3-cycle, the loop plus 2-cycle, the seven-cell state,\n424\t    `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, the mixed-body, the anchored and the two-2-cycle\n425\t    states refuse at `emit` with `:yin.k/non-portable`\n426\t    `:unresolved-cursor-cells`, the paths and cells of appendix A, the\n427\t    same verdict and path after renaming plus shuffling; a carrier of\n428\t    each built with `emit-numbered` decodes to `:yin.k/undecodable\n429\t    :unresolved-cursor-cells` with the path prefixed by the root, never\n430\t    `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; a parent whose install `'a` holds the\n431\t    2-cycle refuses at `emit` with path `[12 a 15]` and its\n432\t    `emit-numbered` carrier decodes to the `:yin.k/undecodable` kind with\n433\t    path `[<root> 12 a 15]`.\n434\t17. `rows-test/canonical-roots-are-pinned`: `fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`\n435\t    unchanged; `a850fce6…` (`[#{X Y} X]`), `4993b5b1…`, `536c810b…`,\n436\t    `e67405ec…` (`[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]`), `6e7c7a6b…` (ten cells) added;\n437\t    `55ff168e…` removed.\n438\t18. `rows-test/counterexample-search`: a seeded generator as in appendix\n439\t    A (at least 2000 random value states, 200 frame-bearing, 100 with\n440\t    install children) in CI: every numbered state's carrier decodes and\n441\t    re-emits to its root; renaming every cursor cell plus shuffling every\n442\t    set, map and census gives an equal root, or the same refusal verdict\n443\t    and path; refusals are counted and reported; the largest `:work` is\n444\t    reported.\n445\t19. `rows-test/numbering-is-total`: a census cell the walk does not reach\n446\t    raises the totality defect, not a refusal, before any row is emitted.\n447\t20. `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `alias-topology-and-census`,\n448\t    `canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant`,\n449\t    `unordered-containers-are-order-free`,\n450\t    `child-scope-and-shared-child-dag` and\n451\t    `census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells` pass unchanged; the\n452\t    rewritten tests of §4 pass.\n453\t21. `rows-test/gates-and-work`: 65 cells refuse `:resource-limit\n454\t    [:cursor-cells]` under the default budget and 2 cells under\n455\t    `{:cursor-cells 1}`; `[[[[X]]]]` under `{:depth 3}` refuses\n456\t    `[:depth]`; a state whose bodies carry a 65536-character descriptor\n457\t    numbers under the default budget; `{:work 1}` refuses `[:work]` at\n458\t    `emit` and, for a valid carrier, at `decode`.\n459\t22. Budgets and docs: `:label-bytes` and `:search` gone, `:work` default\n460\t    `4194304`; A1–A9 of §6 applied; kondo clean; `bb test:sub yin.vm`\n461\t    green on three hosts; Node log shows `Testing yin.vm.ucf.rows-test`.\n462\t\n463\t## 6. Amendments to the carrier and continuation documents\n464\t\n465\t**A1 — carrier §6, from \"The procedure.\" to the end of step 5.** Replace\n466\twith §1 above. Keep the section's first paragraph (the counterexample)\n467\tand amend \"What is defined, and what is not claimed\" to: \"The canonical\n468\tnumbering is defined as the output of the discovery-order procedure\n469\tbelow, a function of the state up to renaming of cursor cells and host\n470\tenumeration of guest sets, maps and the census (proof in\n471\t`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md` §2). It is not the\n472\tminimum of the root bytes over all bijections. The one choice the walk\n473\tcannot make by itself, between tied members of one guest set or map that\n474\tthe tie test cannot prove interchangeable, is refused\n475\t(`:unresolved-cursor-cells`); the refusal certifies nothing about\n476\tinterchangeability and can be narrowed later without changing any\n477\tnumbering assigned today.\"\n478\t\n479\t**A2 — carrier §6 \"Validation\".** Replace the paragraph's last two\n480\tsentences, from \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is\n481\tthereby accepted, and no encounter-order tie-break ever decides validity\"\n482\tto the end, with: \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is\n483\tthereby accepted. The emitted commitment and the acceptance decision are\n484\tinvariant under the host's enumeration order of every set, map and\n485\tcensus (numbering note §2); the procedure's one use of host order,\n486\tentering the members of a tie group proved interchangeable, cannot\n487\tchange the rows, and a receiver never decides validity by its own\n488\tenumeration order. This is a lower-side structural refusal, not a lift\n489\tkind. A receiver whose work bound (§10.10) is exceeded before the\n490\tprocedure completes refuses with the structured resource refusal before\n491\tany attachment, never labels the state `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`, and\n492\tnever accepts an unfinished check; a receiver that reaches the tie\n493\trefusal refuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`.\"\n494\t\n495\t**A3 — carrier §5 preamble.** \"The class-erased skeletons of §6 are used\n496\tonly inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order of an\n497\temitted row\" → \"The spellings (keys) of §6 are used only inside the\n498\tnumbering procedure and never decide the order of an emitted row\".\n499\t\n500\t**A4 — carrier §8 step 6.** \"Recompute §6 under the work bound, for the\n501\troot and every install child, and compare (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind\n502\t`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` if the tie\n503\trefusal is reached in the root or in a child, translated from the\n504\trecomputation's lift-side refusal of that kind only, its path prefixed\n505\tby the carried root id; or the resource refusal of §10.10 if the bound\n506\tis exceeded, which propagates untranslated).\" In the diagnostics\n507\tparagraph after step 12 add `:unresolved-cursor-cells` to the\n508\t`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds; \"the two kinds this carrier adds to UCF\n509\t§7.5.4's set: `:byte-string` and `:metadata`\" → \"the three kinds …,\n510\t`:metadata` and `:unresolved-cursor-cells`\"; in §11 \"two lift kinds and\n511\ttwo `:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added\" → \"three … and three …\".\n512\t\n513\t**A5 — carrier §10.** Answer 9: \"a renaming-invariant\n514\trefinement-and-search procedure\" → \"the discovery-order procedure, which\n515\tis invariant under renaming and host enumeration\". Answer 10: \"a bound\n516\ton decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells, traversal depth, refinement work\n517\tand search nodes for §6\" → \"a bound on decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells,\n518\ttraversal depth and traversal-and-spelling work for §6 (its `:work` unit\n519\tcounts positions and representation bytes, charged before any spelling\n520\tis encoded)\"; \"never falls back to encounter order\" → \"never substitutes\n521\thost enumeration order for the canonical walk\". Proposals list: \"as\n522\tdefined by its procedure\" → \"as defined by discovery order, and the\n523\t`:unresolved-cursor-cells` refusal\".\n524\t\n525\t**A6 — carrier §3, identity rows.** After \"minted by the lift under §6's\n526\tcanonical numbering\" add \"(the n-th cursor cell met in §6's canonical\n527\twalk)\".\n528\t\n529\t**A7 — continuation §2.2.** \"numbered by the draft's §6 procedure\" →\n530\t\"numbered by discovery order (draft §6 as amended by\n531\t`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`)\".\n532\t\n533\t**A8 — continuation §5.5.** (i) \"… and the only other identity it\n534\tconsumes is the stream marker in each cursor cell's initial label\n535\t(`[:body <stream-marker-bytes> <position-bytes>]`, draft §6 step 1) and\n536\tin cursor-cell bodies\" → \"… and the only other identity it consumes is\n537\tthe stream marker in each cursor cell's body label (`[:body [:stream\n538\tidentity descriptor] position]`, draft §6 as amended, spelled by rank\n539\tinside keys) and in cursor-cell bodies\". (ii) \"Unordered containers\n540\t(`:set`, `:map`, `:store`, `:bindings`, the parked table) are encoded by\n541\tsorting their elements' encodings bytewise (draft §6 step 2), never by\n542\thost traversal order\" → \"Guest sets and maps are entered in the bytewise\n543\torder of their members' keys, and `:store`, `:bindings` and the parked\n544\ttable in the byte order of their structural keys (draft §6 W1), never in\n545\thost enumeration order\". (iii) \"never derived from traversal order\" →\n546\t\"never derived from *host* traversal order (the canonical walk of draft\n547\t§6 is a function of the state and is what the procedure reads)\".\n548\t\n549\t**A9 — continuation §8.1 acceptance.** Item 10 stands; append items\n550\t12–22 of §5.\n551\t\n552\t**A10 — UCF §7.5.3.** No change.\n553\t\n554\t## 7. Owner questions\n555\t\n556\t1. The key spells each unnumbered cell's **occurrence count** besides its\n557\t   body rank and local index (§1.1). This is one integer per cell from\n558\t   one pass; it is what numbers the path and `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` without a\n559\t   search and what makes the review counterexample, `[#{X Y} X]`, the\n560\t   codex A/B states and the witness reach 6a's own roots. Appendix A\n561\t   measures its effect (295 of 1000 tie-heavy states, 20 of 2000 random\n562\t   ones numbered only because of it; nothing lost). Keep, or drop to the\n563\t   literal \"body plus local index\" (a one-line change, fewer states\n564\t   numbered, different pins)?\n565\t2. `:work` default `4194304`, replacing 6a's `200000` positions plus\n566\t   `1048576` label bytes by one budget that counts both. Agreed?\n567\t\n568\t---\n569\t\n570\t# Appendices (non-normative)\n571\t\n572\t## A. Prototype and measurements (round 4)\n573\t\n574\t`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj` (scratch project under\n575\t`collab/`, never committed) implements §1 exactly: one walk procedure\n576\tused for the root walk and for key walks, memoized on (member, numbering\n577\trestricted to the member's cells, local counter); the gates and the\n578\tcharging of §1.5; the totality assertion; an emitter that numbers install\n579\tchildren by the same procedure; a decode path that runs 6a's `decode*`\n580\twith its numbering check off and wraps the whole discovery recomputation\n581\tin §1.4's translation. Two diagnostic switches: `:permissive` (a failing\n582\tgroup is entered in listed order, one group at a time, instead of\n583\trefusing; used only to decide whether a refused state is in fact\n584\tinterchangeable, by trying every listed order of every container when\n585\tthere are at most 720 such orders, else 48 samples) and `:count-spelling\n586\tfalse` (the occurrence count left out of the key). The round-3 prototype\n587\tis kept unchanged as `probe/discovery_r3.clj` for appendix B. Run from\n588\tthe probe directory (`run.sh`; JVM only; the 6a copies there match\n589\t`srvm-phase6a` by SHA-1):\n590\t\n591\t```\n592\tmise exec -- java -Xmx2g @java.args clojure.main -e \"(require 'probe.discovery) (probe.discovery/-main)\"\n593\t```\n594\t\n595\tResults of 2026-10-12, round 4 (`collab/numbering-probe/run.log`; 6a's\n596\town tests pass unchanged in the same project, 21 tests, 324 assertions).\n597\tRoots marked (6a) equal 6a's root for the same state.\n598\t\n599\t| State | Numbering | Root | `:work` |\n600\t|---|---|---|---:|\n601\t| `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y` (`rows_test.cljc:28`) | `x cc-1, y cc-2` | `fab223…` (6a) | 37210 |\n602\t| review counterexample `'a #{X Y}`, `'b X` | `y cc-1, x cc-2` | `2d9873a8…` (6a) | 3455 |\n603\t| `#{X Y}` | `x cc-1, y cc-2` | `07eda35d…` (6a) | 3185 |\n604\t| `[x x y]` / `[x y y]` (`:159`) | `x cc-1, y cc-2` | `4993b5b1…` / `536c810b…` (6a) | 1275 |\n605\t| `#{[X X] [Y Y]}` / `{X 1, Y 1}` | `x cc-1, y cc-2` | `da69c679…` (6a) / `49d4478c…` (6a) | 6197 / 5107 |\n606\t| codex A `#{[X X] [Y Z]}`, both enumerations | `y cc-1, z cc-2, x cc-3` | `387f294a…` (6a) | 6198 |\n607\t| codex B `{X X, Y Z}` | `y cc-1, z cc-2, x cc-3` | `be62ba43…` (6a) | 6212 |\n608\t| codex C `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`, and renamed | first listed inner set `cc-1, cc-2` | `18b70de6…` (6a) | 13831 |\n609\t| `[#{X Y} X]` | `y cc-1, x cc-2` | `a850fce6…` (6a) | 3189 |\n610\t| `[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]` | `y cc-1, x cc-2, z cc-3, w cc-4` | `e67405ec…` | 13835 |\n611\t| witness `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]` | `b cc-1, a cc-2` | `0193312…` (6a) | 16564 |\n612\t| `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` | `x cc-1, y cc-2, z cc-3` | `d84480d4…` | 4848 |\n613\t| path `#{[A B] [B C]}` | `a cc-1, b cc-2, c cc-3` | `726a9fbb…` | 6199 |\n614\t| ten cells in a vector (`:331`) | `cc-1 … cc-10` in order | `6e7c7a6b…` (6a) | 12413 |\n615\t| frame fixture: wait window `x`, K-frame window `y`, environment `y`, parked K-frame window `#{x y}` | `y cc-1` (free environment, slot 5), `x cc-2` (parked table, slot 8), `:test/cursor cc-3`; decodes | `0b9ef61e…` | 3836 |\n616\t| frame fixture: wait window `#{x y}`, parked K-frame window `y` | `y cc-1, x cc-2, :test/cursor cc-3`; decodes | `ff124bfc…` | 3122 |\n617\t| parent with two installs sharing the review-counterexample child | child `y cc-1, x cc-2`; one child row shared; decodes | `09124ab9…` | — |\n618\t| 400000-element literal beside a cell in a vector | numbers | `be7fc44e…` | 1273 |\n619\t\n620\tRefused (all `:yin.k/non-portable :unresolved-cursor-cells`; verdict\n621\tand path equal over 24 renamings plus shuffles; \"interchangeable\" is the\n622\texhaustive permissive verdict):\n623\t\n624\t| State | Path | Cells | Reason | Interchangeable | 6a |\n625\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n626\t| 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}` | `[15]` | `a b` | shared within group | yes | `0fe898cc…` |\n627\t| 3-cycle | `[15]` | `a b c` | shared within group | yes | `0b181cd4…` |\n628\t| loop plus 2-cycle (`:312`) | `[15]` | `a b` | shared within group | yes | `55ff168e…` |\n629\t| seven-cell triangle plus square (`:392`) | `[15]` | `a … g` | shared within group | **no** | `[:label-bytes]` |\n630\t| `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` | `[15 0]` | `x y` | shared across groups | yes | `92747c0f…` |\n631\t| mixed bodies `#{[A B] [A C]}` | `[15]` | `a b c` | shared within group | yes | numbered |\n632\t| anchored `[#{…} A]` | `[15 0]` | `b c d e` | shared across groups | yes | numbered |\n633\t| `#{#{[A B] [B A]} #{[C D] [D C]}}` | `[15]` | `a b c d` | incomplete key | yes | `[:label-bytes]` |\n634\t| `[#{#{X Y}} #{[X Z] [Y W]}]` | `[15 0 :member]` | `w x y z` | shared across groups | yes | numbered |\n635\t| `#{[#{X Y} #{X Y}] [#{P Q} #{R S}]}` | `[15 :member 0]` | `x y` | shared across groups | yes | `[:label-bytes]` |\n636\t| install child holding the 2-cycle | lift `[12 a 15]`; decode of an `emit-numbered` carrier: `:yin.k/undecodable`, path `[<root> 12 a 15]`, cells `[cc-1 cc-2]` | | | | |\n637\t\n638\tGates: two cells under `{:cursor-cells 1}` and 65 cells under the\n639\tdefault budget refuse `[:cursor-cells]`; `[[[[X]]]]` under `{:depth 3}`\n640\trefuses `[:depth]`; `{:work 1}` refuses `[:work]` at lift and, for a\n641\tvalid carrier, at decode; the 400000-element literal inside a set refuses\n642\t`[:work]`; an unreachable census cell is the defect\n643\t`{:missing [:x], :extra []}`. The 65536-character descriptor state\n644\tnumbers at `:work` 544554 (21290 at 128 characters; encoded comparison\n645\tbytes 132540 against 1718, all charged before encoding).\n646\t\n647\tRandom search (seeded; each state compared with itself renamed and\n648\tshuffled, and decoded through 6a's decoder, whose own retained\n649\trecomputation budget stops 397 of the carriers at `[:label-bytes]`: a\n650\tprobe limitation, not a failure):\n651\t\n652\t| Generator | Numbered | Refused (interch. / not / undecided) | Only across groups | Count gain | Invariance fail | Decode ok / fail | max `:work` |\n653\t|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n654\t| random values, 2000 | 1981 | 19 (16 / 3 / 0) | 7 | 20 | 0 | 1789 / 0 | 624926 |\n655\t| frame-bearing, 200 | 196 | 4 (3 / 1 / 0) | 0 | 4 | 0 | 179 / 0 | 202939 |\n656\t| with children, 100 | 98 | 2 (1 / 0 / 1) | 1 | 0 | 0 | 75 / 0 | 434068 |\n657\t| incidence families, 1000 | 832 | 168 (92 / 17 / 59) | 74 | 295 | 0 | 667 / 0 | 453009 |\n658\t\n659\t\"Only across groups\" counts refusals whose every failing group failed\n660\tonly clause (iii): an upper bound on what the component rule of appendix\n661\tB could recover. \"Count gain\" counts states numbered only because the\n662\tkey carries the occurrence count; the reverse count is 0 everywhere. A\n663\ttest that simply entered ties in host order would have accepted the 21\n664\tstates classified not interchangeable, with host-order-dependent rows:\n665\tthat is why a sound test is needed at all.\n666\t\n667\tSize: the prototype's procedure region (`discovery.clj:79-416`) is 296\n668\tnon-blank lines, of which roughly 80 are docstrings, trace and the two\n669\tdiagnostic switches; 6a's `canonical-numbering` plus\n670\t`check-label-bytes!` is 136 non-blank lines. In `rows.cljc` the\n671\treplacement reuses `descriptors`, `bytes-key`, `compare-bytes`, `spend!`,\n672\t`refuse!`, the `size` bound and `emit-node`; expect about 200 added or\n673\treplacement lines and about 135 deleted, excluding tests.\n674\t\n675\t## B. Round 3's variants: components (D4), conflicts (D5) and the residue search (D6). Optional extension, not adopted\n676\t\n677\tRound 3 (`collab/1792220000000-numbering-note.partial-round4.md`, 980\n678\tlines, and `probe/discovery_r3.clj`) resolved surviving ties differently:\n679\tlink tied members that share an unnumbered cell into **components**;\n680\tencode each component by a local run over a synthetic\n681\t`[:component g₁ i₁ g₂ i₂ …]` row; enter the components of least\n682\tencoding; a component holding two members of one group is a **conflict**,\n683\trefused (D5) or resolved by **trials** (D6): one local run per member of\n684\tthe duplicated group with that member entered first, the least complete\n685\tspelling adopted, charged to `:search`. The base design of §1 keeps none\n686\tof it: no component runs, no trials, no `:search` budget. What the\n687\textension would add over the base: `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` and the other\n688\t\"shared across groups\" refusals (at most 82 of 3300 generated states,\n689\tappendix A) via components, and with D6 every state of appendix A's\n690\trefused table, including the seven-cell state (11 trials) and 6a's own\n691\troots on the 2-cycle and 3-cycle.\n692\t\n693\t*Proof sketch (round 3, lemmas C and E).* Equal complete component\n694\tencodings are faithful spellings and exhibit a bijection under which the\n695\tcomponents are the same structure group by group; since the cells of\n696\tdistinct components are disjoint and confined, the block swap is an\n697\tautomorphism, so entering a run of equal encodings in host order gives\n698\tthe same rows. For D6, the multiset of trial spellings is canonical if\n699\teach trial is, and equal least spellings give isomorphic numberings. The\n700\tthird review found the induction incomplete: a trial starts from the\n701\tsame numbering and the same cells as its parent, the forced member need\n702\tnot number a cell before recursive work begins, and key runs under D6\n703\tsearch instead of spelling residue; a well-founded measure covering key\n704\truns, synthetic component runs, forced traversal and recursive trials\n705\twas never supplied. That, with the reviewer's judgement that D6 is not\n706\tmaterially simpler than 6a, is why it is not adopted.\n707\t\n708\t*Measurements (round 3, `collab/numbering-probe/run-r3.log`).* Under D6:\n709\t2000 random value states, 200 frame-bearing and 100 with children all\n710\tnumbered, 0 invariance failures, 0 decode failures, at most 13 trials\n711\tand 1470 `:work` units (round-3 accounting, positions only); the 2-cycle\n712\tand 3-cycle reached 6a's roots (`0fe898cc…`, `0b181cd4…`), the loop plus\n713\t2-cycle `858a891e…`, the seven-cell state `d76d787a…` in 11 trials and\n714\t488 units; the reviewer's independent 5000-state run found no\n715\tcanonicality counterexample in either mode and 2117 D5 refusals.\n716\t\n717\t## C. Why not portable ids minted in the VM\n718\t\n719\tThe owner asked whether a cursor cell could carry a portable id from\n720\tcreation. Every candidate (branch `srvm-phase6a`,\n721\t`src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc`: the task's id counter `gen-id` `:227-230`\n722\tand `gensym` `:2312-2318`; the creation sequence of `:stream/cursor`\n723\texecutions, `handle-cursor-unminted` `:557-568`, minted at `:2595-2602`;\n724\ta hash of occurrence and counter; a random id) names history, not\n725\tstructure. Two tasks with structurally equal snapshots (two cursor cells\n726\twith equal bodies at equal positions, equal counters) carry different\n727\tid sets when one created and dropped a third cursor and the other\n728\tconsumed an unrelated fresh name; the cross-engine law would then make\n729\tan execution trace part of the frame's identity, and lower would have to\n730\tkeep a second global name beside its private resource entry. Discovery\n731\torder gives the identity a structural name instead. Recommended against.\n732\t\n733\t## D. History\n734\t\n735\tRound 1 (2026-10-12): discovery order proposed; the orbit lemma was\n736\tunsound (`#{[X X] [Y Z]}`) and nested ties were never processed\n737\t(`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`). Round 2: alias-carrying keys, the fixpoint, the\n738\tcomponent rule; the review found speculative key-run conflicts refusing\n739\twrongly and an install child's refusal escaping decode. Round 3: key\n740\truns never refuse, the child translation, rank spelling, the `:work`\n741\tbudget, D6; the review found the byte accounting unimplemented, the D6\n742\tinduction incomplete, the coverage paragraph wrong, the `:cursor-cells`\n743\tand `:depth` gates dropped and the large-literal test incompatible with\n744\ttotality. Round 4 (this text): the base design, with every one of those\n745\tfindings addressed in §§1–4 and the variants moved here.\n746\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"704437a3-7e24-494b-ab26-b13fa72d3d40","uuid":"d2dd7a6e-ca41-4b34-9178-e838b2bf1194","timestamp":"2026-10-12T11:43:13.027Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-numbering/docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md","content":"# Cursor-cell numbering by discovery order (companion to the row carrier)\n\nStatus: **Adopted by the owner 2026-10-12 (replaces carrier §6's\nrefinement and search); not yet implemented.** Round 4 of the same day\n(Architect, claude-fable-5-1): the base design the owner asked for after\nthe third review (`collab/1792210000000-…review-r3…findings.md`), with\nthe round-3 variants moved to appendix B as an extension that is not\nadopted. Subordinate to\n[`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md) (the\ncarrier, body version 3) and\n[`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md`](./yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md);\nvocabulary is UCF §7.5.3 (\"Terminology: cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref,\ncell\") exactly. Evidence is slice 6a's `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc`\nand `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc` (branch `srvm-phase6a` at\n`d77da9f0`) and the prototype of appendix A. §§1–6 are normative; the\nappendices are not.\n\n**The rule in one paragraph.** A cursor cell is named by the order in\nwhich a fixed walk over the task meets it: the n-th cursor cell met is\n`:yin.k/cc-n`, as a de Bruijn index names a binder by position. The walk\nis pre-order in the carrier's table order, so every position is ordered\nexcept the members of a guest set and the entries of a guest map. Those\nthe carrier *transforms* into ordered vectors before numbering: each\nmember is given a key, the spelling of the member in which cursor cells\nalready numbered appear by number and the others by body, occurrence\ncount and the order in which they first appear inside the member; the\nmembers are sorted by key. Members whose keys are equal are *tied*; they\nare postponed until the rest of the walk has numbered what it can, and\nre-keyed. A tie that survives is entered in any order if a cheap test\nproves its members interchangeable (swapping them leaves the whole state\nunchanged); otherwise the lift refuses. Guest programs are unrestricted:\nsets and maps may hold cursor-refs anywhere, including as map keys.\n\n**Example.** `[P Q P]` numbers `P = cc-1`, `Q = cc-2` whatever the bodies.\nIn `{'a #{X Y}, 'b X}` with equal bodies, the set is reached first; `X`\noccurs twice in the task and `Y` once, so their keys differ and `Y` is\nentered first: `Y = cc-1`, `X = cc-2` (the root is 6a's, `2d9873a8…`).\nIn `#{[A B] [B A]}` the two pairs have equal keys and share both cells;\nthe test cannot prove them interchangeable (they are) and the lift\nrefuses `:unresolved-cursor-cells`. In `#{[A B] [B C]}` the keys differ\n(`B` occurs twice) and the path numbers `A, B, C = cc-1, cc-2, cc-3`.\n\n## 1. The rule\n\n**Vocabulary.** An **item** is a row or a value. The **walk** is the\nprocedure of §1.2 over one item from a numbering; the **root walk** is\nover the `:task` row from the empty numbering and its numbers are the\noutput; a **key walk** is the same procedure over one member of a set or\nmap from the enclosing walk's numbering, run only to produce the member's\n**key**. A **member** is a `:set` element or a `:map` entry taken as the\nsynthetic ordered row `[:entry key value]`. A **tie group** is a run of\nmembers of one container with equal keys. A cursor cell's **body rank**\nis the index of its census body label `[:body [:stream identity\ndescriptor] position]` among the census's distinct labels in bytewise\norder; its **occurrence count** is the number of positions of the task\n(census and install children excluded) that hold it. Install children\nare numbered as their own tasks by the same rule, in any order.\n\n### 1.1 The transformation and the key\n\nWhen the walk reaches a `:set` or `:map` it replaces the container, for\nits own purposes, by the vector of its members in key order. The key of\na member is the **spelling** of that member produced by a key walk under\nthe numbering at that moment (every member of one container is keyed\nfrom that one numbering):\n\n- a cursor cell numbered by the enclosing walk spells `[:cursor-cell\n  [:n k]]` (root numbers) or as the enclosing key walk spelled it;\n- a cursor cell the key walk meets unnumbered receives a **local index**\n  `j` (the order of first meeting, counted from the enclosing walk's\n  local counter so a nested key walk never reuses an index of its\n  enclosing one) and spells `[:cursor-cell [:u j r m]]`, `r` its body\n  rank and `m` its occurrence count, all exact CBOR integers;\n- `[:lit d]` as itself, structural data as itself (by reference, never\n  copied), `[:row B]` expanded in place, the census as `[:cursor-cells]`,\n  an install child as `[:child]`, keyed structural rows in canonical key\n  order, `:window` in register order, and a nested `:set` or `:map` as\n  the bytewise sorted vector of its members' spellings in which a tie\n  group the key walk could not certify (§1.3) is spelled in place as\n  `[:tie K … K]`, its common key once per member.\n\nKeys are compared bytewise on canonical CBOR; the spelling is hash-free.\n`[X X]` keys `[:vec [u0 r m] [u0 r m]]` and `[Y Z]` keys\n`[:vec [u0 r m] [u1 r m']]`: the alias pattern inside a member is part\nof its key, and so is each cell's occurrence count, which separates\n`#{[A B] [B C]}` and `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` without a search (appendix A).\n\n### 1.2 The walk\n\n**W1, order.** Depth-first, pre-order, from the walk's item. A row's\nslots in table order (`rows/row-table`, `rows.cljc:37-57`); ordered\nrepeated slots (`frames`, `:vec`, `:list`, `:k`) in order; keyed rows\nwith structural keys (`:bindings`, `:store`, `:parked-table`, the root's\n`installs` and `module-stores`) in the Jing canonical byte order of the\nkey; `:window` in ascending register order. The census is not entered;\nan install child's `:task` row is not entered. A `:set` or `:map` is\nentered as §1.1 orders it: members with a unique key in key order, an\nentry's key before its value; each tie group is **postponed** to the\nwalk's queue (queue order is canonical: containers in walk order, groups\nwithin one container in key order).\n\n**W2, meeting.** When the walk reaches a `[:cursor-cell C]` value or a\n`C` slot of a `:pending` row whose cursor cell has no number, the cell\nreceives the next number (the root walk counts from 1). A numbered cell\nis passed over. Every occurrence emits `[:cursor-cell :yin.k/cc-n]`; the\ncensus row stays the contents table.\n\n**W3, postponed ties.** After the pre-order walk, the queue is taken in\norder: each group's members are re-keyed under the current numbering and\ntreated as in W1 (unique keys entered in key order, surviving ties\nre-queued in order; a tie met inside an entered member joins the same\nqueue). Repeat while a pass numbers at least one cursor cell and groups\nremain. When a pass numbers nothing, the surviving groups go to the tie\ntest.\n\n### 1.3 The tie test\n\nA surviving group **passes** when all of (i) its common key is complete\n(contains no `[:tie …]`), (ii) the unnumbered cursor cells of its members\nare pairwise disjoint, and (iii) none of them occurs in a member of any\nother surviving group of the same walk. (At this point every unnumbered\ncell occurs only in postponed members, so (ii) and (iii) say: the cells\nof each member are confined to that member.) If every surviving group\npasses, all of them are entered, each group's members in the order the\nhost lists them (§2 shows the rows do not depend on it), and the walk\nreturns to W3. If any group fails, the **root walk refuses** (§1.4) and a\n**key walk stops**: its surviving groups are spelled as `[:tie …]`\nresidue and its key is incomplete. The test inside a key walk reads only\nthe member being keyed; it never reads the enclosing state.\n\nThis is the cheapest sound test: (i)–(iii) are what is needed for the\nindex-matched swap of two members to be an automorphism of the state;\nwhatever it refuses may still be interchangeable (§3 says what), but\nnothing it accepts can be host-order dependent.\n\n### 1.4 Refusal, and decode\n\nThe root walk refuses `:yin.k/non-portable` kind\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells`, `:yin.k/path` the row path of the container\nholding the first failing group in queue order (a container nested under\na member of a set or map has the enclosing container's path followed by\n`:member`, since no index into an unordered container is canonical; for\nan install child the path is prefixed `[12 <install name> …]`), and\n`:yin.k/cursor-cells` the sorted unnumbered cursor cells of the failing\ngroups' members. The verdict and the path are functions of the state up\nto renaming and host enumeration; the cell list names local source ids.\nThis is not a resource refusal and never `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`.\n\nThe receiver decodes, recomputes the numbering of the root and of every\ninstall child, re-emits and compares root ids\n(`:yin.k/undecodable :noncanonical-cursor-cells` on mismatch). In 6a's\n`decode*` the recomputation is the `emit` call (`rows.cljc:570-575`);\nthe **whole** of that call is wrapped so that only a lift-side\n`:yin.k/non-portable :unresolved-cursor-cells` (root or child) becomes\n`:yin.k/undecodable :unresolved-cursor-cells` with `:yin.k/path` prefixed\nby the carried root id (a child's path reads `[<root> 12 <name> …]`); a\nresource refusal propagates as itself; nothing else can arise there,\nsince step 5 has checked literals and markers. The validator order stays:\nnumbering (step 6) after step 5 and before `validate-frames`\n(`rows.cljc:576-578`; test `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`).\n\n### 1.5 Budgets and gates\n\n- `:cursor-cells`: the census size is charged first, before ranks,\n  counts or any walk (6a's gate at `rows.cljc:256`, kept).\n- `:depth`: the walk's nesting; a member step counts one level (6a's\n  gate at `rows.cljc:291-292`, kept, now in the walk).\n- `:work`: one unit per position visited (walk, key walk, cell scans,\n  count and rank preparation) or spelled, one unit per cell in a key\n  walk's memo key, and, **before every CBOR encoding made for a\n  comparison** (keys, rank labels, structural keys), the representation\n  bound of the value encoded, computed by reading it: 6a's `size` of\n  `check-label-bytes!` (`rows.cljc:218-229`), an upper bound of the\n  encoding's bytes. Spellings hold input literals by reference, so their\n  own size is their spelled positions. Hence every byte allocated for a\n  comparison is charged before the allocation, and the bytes alive at\n  any time are bounded by the budget plus the input. No polynomial bound\n  is claimed; the budget is the bound. Default `4194304`, the scale of\n  the deleted `:label-bytes` (appendix A: at most 624926 over 3300\n  generated states, 544554 with a 65536-character stream descriptor). A\n  literal of more than about 30000 scalars inside a guest set or map\n  exceeds it (6a allowed about 8000); in an ordered position a literal is\n  never spelled and costs one unit.\n- `:rows`, `:bytes`: unchanged. `:label-bytes` and `:search` are deleted.\n\nExceeding any budget is the resource refusal of carrier §10.10\n(`:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [<key>]`), never a canonicality\nverdict, at lift and at decode alike.\n\n### 1.6 Totality\n\nThe root walk ends with an empty queue or a refusal. Before emission the\nimplementation asserts that the numbering is a bijection from the\ncensus's cursor cells onto `1 … n`; a violation (a census cell no walk\nreaches) is a defect, an exception without `:yin.k/status` carrying the\nmissing and extra cells, never a refusal and never a partially numbered\ncarrier. 6a's test `input-row-bodies-do-not-spend-label-bytes`\n(`rows_test.cljc:406-421`) declares exactly such a cell in `base` and\nexpects `emit` to succeed (`:414`); §4 rewrites it.\n\n## 2. Proof of canonicality\n\nCall two configurations (abstract task, numbering, queue) **isomorphic**\nwhen a bijection `π` of cursor cells carries one to the other up to the\nhost order of every set, map and census, with `N'(π(c)) = N(c)` and\nqueues corresponding group by group. **Theorem:** on isomorphic\nconfigurations the procedure refuses at corresponding points or emits\nbyte-identical rows. The proof is an induction on the measure\n`(size of the walk's item as a sub-term, unnumbered cells of the walk)`.\n\n*Sort.* The key of a member is a function of the member and the\nnumbering up to isomorphism. The key walk reads structure, numbers,\nranks (a function of the census up to isomorphism) and occurrence counts\n(an isomorphism invariant); it orders nested containers by the keys of\ntheir members, canonical by the induction (a member is a proper sub-term,\nso the measure decreases); its only other listed-order input is the\norder in which it enters an interchangeable group, harmless by the tie\nstep below; and what it cannot certify it spells as `[:tie K … K]`, a\nfunction of `K` and the group's size. Equal keys therefore mean\nisomorphic members when the key is complete (a complete spelling is\nfaithful: structure slot by slot, every cell by local index, rank and\ncount, so matching indices is a bijection of the members' cells under\nwhich the members agree).\n\n*Walk.* W1 and W2 read only table order and keys; W3 repeats W1 on the\nqueue in a canonical order. So the numbering before the tie test, the\nsurviving groups and their keys are functions of the configuration up to\nisomorphism, and so is the verdict of the test, whose inputs (key\ncompleteness, the sets of unnumbered cells of members) are structural.\n\n*Tie step.* Let `G` pass with members `e_1 … e_k`. Their keys are equal\nand complete, so for each pair there is an index-matched bijection\n`π_ij` of their unnumbered cells carrying `e_i` to `e_j`; the cells of\ndistinct members are disjoint (ii) and occur nowhere else in the state\n(iii), so the permutation composed from these bijections, extended by\nthe identity, is an automorphism of the whole state that permutes the\nmembers of `G` and fixes everything else. Entering `G` in two host orders\n`σ`, `σ'` gives configurations related by that automorphism: numbers\nagree by construction (the cells of the t-th entered member receive the\nsame block, by local index), structures agree, queues agree. The rest of\nthe procedure is equivariant by the theorem's induction, so the rows are\nthe same. Passing groups are mutually independent by (iii), so entering\nall of them before the next pass changes nothing.\n\n*Emission.* Cells are emitted by number only and every unordered slot is\nsorted by canonical bytes under the final numbering (carrier §5), so\nisomorphic final configurations emit the same rows. ∎\n\n*Termination.* There is no search and no re-entry: the only recursion is\nthe key walk, over a proper sub-term of its caller's item, so key walks\nnest at most as deep as the structure (`:depth`). Within one walk, every\nW3 pass except the last numbers a cell, and every tie step either numbers\na cell or replaces the queued members by proper sub-terms of themselves\n(members with no unnumbered cell are equal values, which the carrier\nrefuses as duplicates at §8 step 5). The measure decreases at each step\nand the budget bounds the count.\n\nConsequences: a function of structure, invariant under renaming and host\nenumeration (the theorem with `π` a renaming, a permutation of\ncontainers, or both); deterministic, so two lifts of one state are\nbyte-identical and the receiver's recomputation is well defined; sharing\nsurvives (one number per cell, every occurrence spells it); the\ncross-engine law of continuation §2.1 follows as before.\n\n## 3. What is refused\n\nRefused iff, when the root walk's last pass numbers nothing, some\nsurviving tie group fails §1.3. Concretely one of:\n\n- **(i) an incomplete key**: a tied member contains a container whose\n  own ties the key walk could not certify. `#{#{[A B] [B A]} #{[C D]\n  [D C]}}` (interchangeable; refused because equal residue keys need not\n  mean isomorphic members: `#{[A B] [B A]}` and `#{[A B] [C D]}` key\n  alike).\n- **(ii) two tied members share a cell**: the 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}`,\n  the 3-cycle, the loop plus 2-cycle `#{[L L] [A B] [B A]}`\n  (`rows_test.cljc:312-329`, where `L` is separated by its key, `A B`\n  are not), the mixed-body `#{[A B] [A C]}` with `A` on another stream\n  position than `B, C` (all interchangeable and refused), and the\n  seven-cell triangle plus square (`rows_test.cljc:392-397`), which is\n  **not** interchangeable: a triangle edge entered first and a square\n  edge entered first give different rows, so no sound test can accept it\n  without a search.\n- **(iii) a tied member's cell occurs in a tied member of another\n  container**: `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, `[#{#{X Y}} #{[X Z] [Y W]}]`, and the\n  reviewer's anchored state `[#{[A B] [A C] [B D] [C E] [D B] [E C]} A]`\n  (`A` is numbered by its ordered position, which does not break the tie\n  of the rest; all three interchangeable and refused).\n\nEqual bodies are not required (the mixed-body case) and an ordered anchor\ndoes not certify a whole component (the anchored case). 6a numbers the\n2-cycle, the 3-cycle, the loop plus 2-cycle, `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, the\nmixed and the anchored states, and refuses the seven-cell state and the\ntwo-2-cycle state at `[:label-bytes]`. Discovery order numbers what 6a\nrefuses at `[:label-bytes]` when the keys separate the members, and\nnumbers the path and `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]`, which 6a also numbers.\n\nMeasured (appendix A; refusals classified by entering the ties in every\nlisted order of every container when there are at most 720 such orders,\nelse 48 sampled orders):\n\n| Generator | States | Numbered | Refused | of which interchangeable | not interchangeable | undecided |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| random values (1–6 cells, two bodies, nested sets, maps with cursor-ref keys, edge sets, sets of sets) | 2000 | 1981 | 19 | 16 | 3 | 0 |\n| frame-bearing (windows, K frames, environments, parked state) | 200 | 196 | 4 | 3 | 1 | 0 |\n| with install children | 100 | 98 | 2 | 1 | 0 | 1 |\n| reviewer's tie-heavy incidence families | 1000 | 832 | 168 | 92 | 17 | 59 |\n\nEvery refusal was (ii) or (iii); none was (i). Invariance failures and\ndecode failures: 0 of 3300. No production corpus exists yet (no\nversion-3 lift before 6b), so these are generator rates, not frequencies.\n\n**Fixtures.** Every state named in this section and in §1's example is a\nfixture of §5 (items 12–16), including the refused ones.\n\n## 4. Replacing 6a: deleted, rewritten, kept\n\nAgainst `srvm-phase6a` (`d77da9f0`), `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` and\n`test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc` (line numbers of those files).\n\n**rows.cljc, deleted.** `check-label-bytes!` (`:212-230`) as a bound on\nlabels (its `size` function, `:218-229`, survives as the representation\nbound of §1.5); inside `canonical-numbering` (`:236-355`): the label\nseeding (`:252-255`), `ce-slot`/`ce` (`:257-284`), `positions`\n(`:285-325`) except its depth gate, `classes` (`:326-330`), `refine`\n(`:331-337`), `search` (`:338-354`), the `checked-*` wrappers\n(`:245-251`); in `default-budget` (`:200-202`) the keys `:label-bytes`\nand `:search`.\n\n**rows.cljc, rewritten.**\n- `canonical-numbering` (`:236-355`), same signature and contract\n  (`task`, optional `budget` → `{source-id :yin.k/cc-n}`; empty census\n  → `{}`): the census gate (`:256`) first; body ranks and occurrence\n  counts; then the root walk of §1.2 with its queue, the tie test, the\n  refusal and the totality assertion, as in the prototype's sections\n  \"cell scans\", \"runs\", \"spelling\" and \"procedure\"\n  (`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj:79-416`), charging as\n  §1.5 says. Portable `.cljc` per continuation §12: no `@#'` reflection;\n  `mapv`/`into`/`reduce`, not `for`, over sequences that may exceed 32\n  elements (the prototype's `group-failure` uses `for`: replace);\n  `(assoc {} …)` never `(assoc nil …)`; atoms are fine on all three\n  hosts. 6a's `bytes-key` (`:91-94`) takes encoded bytes; the prototype's\n  takes a value: keep 6a's and encode first.\n- `descriptors` (`:117-124`): reused, with the synthetic `:entry` row\n  added (`[:V :V]`).\n- `default-budget` (`:200-202`): `:work` default `4194304`.\n- `emit-node` (`:181-197`): thread a row path so a child's refusal\n  reports `[12 <install name> …]`.\n- `decode*` recompute (`:570-575`): the translation of §1.4 around the\n  whole `emit` call.\n- Namespace docstring (`:1-17`): \"canonical cursor-cell numbering\" →\n  \"discovery-order cursor-cell numbering (design note §1)\".\n\n**rows.cljc, kept unchanged.** `row-table`, `pending-table`, `node`,\n`refuse!`/`bad!`, the marker checks, `bytes-key`/`compare-bytes`/\n`byte-compare`/`sorted-bytes`, the census order (`:137-156`),\n`emit-slot`/`emit-node` apart from the path, `spend!` (`:205-209`),\n`emit-numbered` (`:358-363`), `emit` (`:366-372`), `structural?`,\n`literal?`, `check-abstract!`, all of `decode*` before `:570` and after\n`:575`, `decode`, `canonical-under`, `frame->rows`, `rows->frame`,\n`validate-frames`, and the `:bytes`, `:rows`, `:depth`, `:cursor-cells`\nkeys with their defaults.\n\n**rows_test.cljc, kept.** `task-node`, `cursor-cell-body`, `fixture`\n(`:16-37`), `portable-frame` and `bijection`, `row-ids-are-jing-default`,\n`replace-row`, `window-sorted-and-unique`,\n`canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant` (`:143-156`),\n`alias-topology-and-census` (`:159-168`), `gates-and-graph-before-hashes`,\n`numbering-precedes-frame-defect` (`:206-213`),\n`schema-and-literal-canonicality`, `child-scope-and-shared-child-dag`\n(`:240-249`), `pending-envelope-and-nil-literal-are-preserved`,\n`task-root-checks-carried-code-and-frame`, `reversed` and\n`unordered-containers-are-order-free` (`:285-304`),\n`census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells` (`:331-338`), the marker tests\n(`:341-381`), `cursor-cell-and-identity-shaped-literal-are-distinct`\n(`:384-389`), the fixture `seven-cursor-cell-triangle-square`\n(`:392-397`).\n\n**rows_test.cljc, rewritten.**\n- `refinement-search-on-symmetric-cursor-cells-is-bounded` (`:171-178`)\n  → `symmetric-cursor-cells-are-interchangeable`: the two assertions on\n  equal roots and two numbers stay; the `{:search 1}` assertions become\n  `{:work 1}` → `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit [:work]`.\n- `canonical-roots-are-pinned` (`:307-328`): `fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`\n  stay; the loop plus 2-cycle block (`:312-328`) asserts the\n  `:unresolved-cursor-cells` refusal with path `[15]` and cells `[:a :b]`\n  for the task and the same verdict and path for `renamed`; the\n  `{:search 2}` assertion and the pin `55ff168e…` go.\n- `intermediate-label-bytes-are-bounded` (`:400-403`) →\n  `seven-cursor-cells-are-refused`: `:yin.k/non-portable\n  :unresolved-cursor-cells`, path `[15]`, cells `[:a … :g]`, and the\n  renamed and shuffled state gives the same verdict and path.\n- `input-row-bodies-do-not-spend-label-bytes` (`:406-421`) →\n  `large-literals-in-ordered-positions-are-free`: `base` (its census\n  cell `:x` is unreachable) now asserts the totality defect of §1.6 (an\n  exception whose data is `{:missing [:x] :extra []}` and has no\n  `:yin.k/status`) in place of `(is (nil? (error #(rows/emit base))))`\n  (`:414`); `reachable` and `custody` keep emitting and decoding to the\n  expected tasks (`:415-421`); add: the same 400000-element literal\n  inside a guest set refuses `:yin.k/unsupported :resource-limit\n  [:work]` under the default budget.\n\n## 5. Acceptance for the rewritten 6a numbering\n\n12. `rows-test/discovery-order-numbering`: `canonical-numbering` returns\n    the maps of appendix A for `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y`, the review\n    counterexample, `[x x y]`, `[x y y]`, `[#{X Y} X]`, the witness\n    `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]`, `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]`, the path and the two\n    frame fixtures.\n13. `rows-test/occurrence-count-separates-members`: the review\n    counterexample numbers `Y cc-1, X cc-2`, pinned `2d9873a8…` (6a's\n    root); the path pinned `726a9fbb…`; `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` pinned\n    `d84480d4…`; each equal after renaming plus reversal of every set.\n14. `rows-test/interchangeable-ties`: `#{X Y}`, `#{[X X] [Y Y]}`,\n    `{X 1, Y 1}`, `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}` number without refusal; reversal and\n    renaming give equal roots; pins `07eda35d…`, `da69c679…`,\n    `49d4478c…`, `18b70de6…`.\n15. `rows-test/alias-pattern-is-part-of-the-key`: `#{[X X] [Y Z]}` and\n    `{X X, Y Z}` give one root for both enumerations and after renaming,\n    pinned `387f294a…` and `be62ba43…` (6a's roots); the witness numbers\n    `B cc-1, A cc-2`, pinned `0193312…` (6a's root), and decodes.\n16. `rows-test/unresolved-cursor-cells-refuse`: the 2-cycle, the\n    3-cycle, the loop plus 2-cycle, the seven-cell state,\n    `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]`, the mixed-body, the anchored and the two-2-cycle\n    states refuse at `emit` with `:yin.k/non-portable`\n    `:unresolved-cursor-cells`, the paths and cells of appendix A, the\n    same verdict and path after renaming plus shuffling; a carrier of\n    each built with `emit-numbered` decodes to `:yin.k/undecodable\n    :unresolved-cursor-cells` with the path prefixed by the root, never\n    `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; a parent whose install `'a` holds the\n    2-cycle refuses at `emit` with path `[12 a 15]` and its\n    `emit-numbered` carrier decodes to the `:yin.k/undecodable` kind with\n    path `[<root> 12 a 15]`.\n17. `rows-test/canonical-roots-are-pinned`: `fab223…` and `c4a7c7…`\n    unchanged; `a850fce6…` (`[#{X Y} X]`), `4993b5b1…`, `536c810b…`,\n    `e67405ec…` (`[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]`), `6e7c7a6b…` (ten cells) added;\n    `55ff168e…` removed.\n18. `rows-test/counterexample-search`: a seeded generator as in appendix\n    A (at least 2000 random value states, 200 frame-bearing, 100 with\n    install children) in CI: every numbered state's carrier decodes and\n    re-emits to its root; renaming every cursor cell plus shuffling every\n    set, map and census gives an equal root, or the same refusal verdict\n    and path; refusals are counted and reported; the largest `:work` is\n    reported.\n19. `rows-test/numbering-is-total`: a census cell the walk does not reach\n    raises the totality defect, not a refusal, before any row is emitted.\n20. `numbering-precedes-frame-defect`, `alias-topology-and-census`,\n    `canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant`,\n    `unordered-containers-are-order-free`,\n    `child-scope-and-shared-child-dag` and\n    `census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells` pass unchanged; the\n    rewritten tests of §4 pass.\n21. `rows-test/gates-and-work`: 65 cells refuse `:resource-limit\n    [:cursor-cells]` under the default budget and 2 cells under\n    `{:cursor-cells 1}`; `[[[[X]]]]` under `{:depth 3}` refuses\n    `[:depth]`; a state whose bodies carry a 65536-character descriptor\n    numbers under the default budget; `{:work 1}` refuses `[:work]` at\n    `emit` and, for a valid carrier, at `decode`.\n22. Budgets and docs: `:label-bytes` and `:search` gone, `:work` default\n    `4194304`; A1–A9 of §6 applied; kondo clean; `bb test:sub yin.vm`\n    green on three hosts; Node log shows `Testing yin.vm.ucf.rows-test`.\n\n## 6. Amendments to the carrier and continuation documents\n\n**A1 — carrier §6, from \"The procedure.\" to the end of step 5.** Replace\nwith §1 above. Keep the section's first paragraph (the counterexample)\nand amend \"What is defined, and what is not claimed\" to: \"The canonical\nnumbering is defined as the output of the discovery-order procedure\nbelow, a function of the state up to renaming of cursor cells and host\nenumeration of guest sets, maps and the census (proof in\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md` §2). It is not the\nminimum of the root bytes over all bijections. The one choice the walk\ncannot make by itself, between tied members of one guest set or map that\nthe tie test cannot prove interchangeable, is refused\n(`:unresolved-cursor-cells`); the refusal certifies nothing about\ninterchangeability and can be narrowed later without changing any\nnumbering assigned today.\"\n\n**A2 — carrier §6 \"Validation\".** Replace the paragraph's last two\nsentences, from \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is\nthereby accepted, and no encounter-order tie-break ever decides validity\"\nto the end, with: \"Every labeling that yields the selected commitment is\nthereby accepted. The emitted commitment and the acceptance decision are\ninvariant under the host's enumeration order of every set, map and\ncensus (numbering note §2); the procedure's one use of host order,\nentering the members of a tie group proved interchangeable, cannot\nchange the rows, and a receiver never decides validity by its own\nenumeration order. This is a lower-side structural refusal, not a lift\nkind. A receiver whose work bound (§10.10) is exceeded before the\nprocedure completes refuses with the structured resource refusal before\nany attachment, never labels the state `:noncanonical-cursor-cells`, and\nnever accepts an unfinished check; a receiver that reaches the tie\nrefusal refuses `:yin.k/undecodable` kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells`.\"\n\n**A3 — carrier §5 preamble.** \"The class-erased skeletons of §6 are used\nonly inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order of an\nemitted row\" → \"The spellings (keys) of §6 are used only inside the\nnumbering procedure and never decide the order of an emitted row\".\n\n**A4 — carrier §8 step 6.** \"Recompute §6 under the work bound, for the\nroot and every install child, and compare (`:yin.k/undecodable`, kind\n`:noncanonical-cursor-cells`; kind `:unresolved-cursor-cells` if the tie\nrefusal is reached in the root or in a child, translated from the\nrecomputation's lift-side refusal of that kind only, its path prefixed\nby the carried root id; or the resource refusal of §10.10 if the bound\nis exceeded, which propagates untranslated).\" In the diagnostics\nparagraph after step 12 add `:unresolved-cursor-cells` to the\n`:yin.k/undecodable` kinds; \"the two kinds this carrier adds to UCF\n§7.5.4's set: `:byte-string` and `:metadata`\" → \"the three kinds …,\n`:metadata` and `:unresolved-cursor-cells`\"; in §11 \"two lift kinds and\ntwo `:yin.k/undecodable` kinds are added\" → \"three … and three …\".\n\n**A5 — carrier §10.** Answer 9: \"a renaming-invariant\nrefinement-and-search procedure\" → \"the discovery-order procedure, which\nis invariant under renaming and host enumeration\". Answer 10: \"a bound\non decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells, traversal depth, refinement work\nand search nodes for §6\" → \"a bound on decoded bytes, rows, cursor cells,\ntraversal depth and traversal-and-spelling work for §6 (its `:work` unit\ncounts positions and representation bytes, charged before any spelling\nis encoded)\"; \"never falls back to encounter order\" → \"never substitutes\nhost enumeration order for the canonical walk\". Proposals list: \"as\ndefined by its procedure\" → \"as defined by discovery order, and the\n`:unresolved-cursor-cells` refusal\".\n\n**A6 — carrier §3, identity rows.** After \"minted by the lift under §6's\ncanonical numbering\" add \"(the n-th cursor cell met in §6's canonical\nwalk)\".\n\n**A7 — continuation §2.2.** \"numbered by the draft's §6 procedure\" →\n\"numbered by discovery order (draft §6 as amended by\n`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md`)\".\n\n**A8 — continuation §5.5.** (i) \"… and the only other identity it\nconsumes is the stream marker in each cursor cell's initial label\n(`[:body <stream-marker-bytes> <position-bytes>]`, draft §6 step 1) and\nin cursor-cell bodies\" → \"… and the only other identity it consumes is\nthe stream marker in each cursor cell's body label (`[:body [:stream\nidentity descriptor] position]`, draft §6 as amended, spelled by rank\ninside keys) and in cursor-cell bodies\". (ii) \"Unordered containers\n(`:set`, `:map`, `:store`, `:bindings`, the parked table) are encoded by\nsorting their elements' encodings bytewise (draft §6 step 2), never by\nhost traversal order\" → \"Guest sets and maps are entered in the bytewise\norder of their members' keys, and `:store`, `:bindings` and the parked\ntable in the byte order of their structural keys (draft §6 W1), never in\nhost enumeration order\". (iii) \"never derived from traversal order\" →\n\"never derived from *host* traversal order (the canonical walk of draft\n§6 is a function of the state and is what the procedure reads)\".\n\n**A9 — continuation §8.1 acceptance.** Item 10 stands; append items\n12–22 of §5.\n\n**A10 — UCF §7.5.3.** No change.\n\n## 7. Owner questions\n\n1. The key spells each unnumbered cell's **occurrence count** besides its\n   body rank and local index (§1.1). This is one integer per cell from\n   one pass; it is what numbers the path and `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` without a\n   search and what makes the review counterexample, `[#{X Y} X]`, the\n   codex A/B states and the witness reach 6a's own roots. Appendix A\n   measures its effect (295 of 1000 tie-heavy states, 20 of 2000 random\n   ones numbered only because of it; nothing lost). Keep, or drop to the\n   literal \"body plus local index\" (a one-line change, fewer states\n   numbered, different pins)?\n2. `:work` default `4194304`, replacing 6a's `200000` positions plus\n   `1048576` label bytes by one budget that counts both. Agreed?\n\n---\n\n# Appendices (non-normative)\n\n## A. Prototype and measurements (round 4)\n\n`collab/numbering-probe/src/probe/discovery.clj` (scratch project under\n`collab/`, never committed) implements §1 exactly: one walk procedure\nused for the root walk and for key walks, memoized on (member, numbering\nrestricted to the member's cells, local counter); the gates and the\ncharging of §1.5; the totality assertion; an emitter that numbers install\nchildren by the same procedure; a decode path that runs 6a's `decode*`\nwith its numbering check off and wraps the whole discovery recomputation\nin §1.4's translation. Two diagnostic switches: `:permissive` (a failing\ngroup is entered in listed order, one group at a time, instead of\nrefusing; used only to decide whether a refused state is in fact\ninterchangeable, by trying every listed order of every container when\nthere are at most 720 such orders, else 48 samples) and `:count-spelling\nfalse` (the occurrence count left out of the key). The round-3 prototype\nis kept unchanged as `probe/discovery_r3.clj` for appendix B. Run from\nthe probe directory (`run.sh`; JVM only; the 6a copies there match\n`srvm-phase6a` by SHA-1):\n\n```\nmise exec -- java -Xmx2g @java.args clojure.main -e \"(require 'probe.discovery) (probe.discovery/-main)\"\n```\n\nResults of 2026-10-12, round 4 (`collab/numbering-probe/run.log`; 6a's\nown tests pass unchanged in the same project, 21 tests, 324 assertions).\nRoots marked (6a) equal 6a's root for the same state.\n\n| State | Numbering | Root | `:work` |\n|---|---|---|---:|\n| `fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y` (`rows_test.cljc:28`) | `x cc-1, y cc-2` | `fab223…` (6a) | 37210 |\n| review counterexample `'a #{X Y}`, `'b X` | `y cc-1, x cc-2` | `2d9873a8…` (6a) | 3455 |\n| `#{X Y}` | `x cc-1, y cc-2` | `07eda35d…` (6a) | 3185 |\n| `[x x y]` / `[x y y]` (`:159`) | `x cc-1, y cc-2` | `4993b5b1…` / `536c810b…` (6a) | 1275 |\n| `#{[X X] [Y Y]}` / `{X 1, Y 1}` | `x cc-1, y cc-2` | `da69c679…` (6a) / `49d4478c…` (6a) | 6197 / 5107 |\n| codex A `#{[X X] [Y Z]}`, both enumerations | `y cc-1, z cc-2, x cc-3` | `387f294a…` (6a) | 6198 |\n| codex B `{X X, Y Z}` | `y cc-1, z cc-2, x cc-3` | `be62ba43…` (6a) | 6212 |\n| codex C `#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`, and renamed | first listed inner set `cc-1, cc-2` | `18b70de6…` (6a) | 13831 |\n| `[#{X Y} X]` | `y cc-1, x cc-2` | `a850fce6…` (6a) | 3189 |\n| `[#{#{X Y} #{Z W}} X]` | `y cc-1, x cc-2, z cc-3, w cc-4` | `e67405ec…` | 13835 |\n| witness `[#{#{[A B] [B A]}} A]` | `b cc-1, a cc-2` | `0193312…` (6a) | 16564 |\n| `[#{X Y} #{Y Z}]` | `x cc-1, y cc-2, z cc-3` | `d84480d4…` | 4848 |\n| path `#{[A B] [B C]}` | `a cc-1, b cc-2, c cc-3` | `726a9fbb…` | 6199 |\n| ten cells in a vector (`:331`) | `cc-1 … cc-10` in order | `6e7c7a6b…` (6a) | 12413 |\n| frame fixture: wait window `x`, K-frame window `y`, environment `y`, parked K-frame window `#{x y}` | `y cc-1` (free environment, slot 5), `x cc-2` (parked table, slot 8), `:test/cursor cc-3`; decodes | `0b9ef61e…` | 3836 |\n| frame fixture: wait window `#{x y}`, parked K-frame window `y` | `y cc-1, x cc-2, :test/cursor cc-3`; decodes | `ff124bfc…` | 3122 |\n| parent with two installs sharing the review-counterexample child | child `y cc-1, x cc-2`; one child row shared; decodes | `09124ab9…` | — |\n| 400000-element literal beside a cell in a vector | numbers | `be7fc44e…` | 1273 |\n\nRefused (all `:yin.k/non-portable :unresolved-cursor-cells`; verdict\nand path equal over 24 renamings plus shuffles; \"interchangeable\" is the\nexhaustive permissive verdict):\n\n| State | Path | Cells | Reason | Interchangeable | 6a |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2-cycle `#{[A B] [B A]}` | `[15]` | `a b` | shared within group | yes | `0fe898cc…` |\n| 3-cycle | `[15]` | `a b c` | shared within group | yes | `0b181cd4…` |\n| loop plus 2-cycle (`:312`) | `[15]` | `a b` | shared within group | yes | `55ff168e…` |\n| seven-cell triangle plus square (`:392`) | `[15]` | `a … g` | shared within group | **no** | `[:label-bytes]` |\n| `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` | `[15 0]` | `x y` | shared across groups | yes | `92747c0f…` |\n| mixed bodies `#{[A B] [A C]}` | `[15]` | `a b c` | shared within group | yes | numbered |\n| anchored `[#{…} A]` | `[15 0]` | `b c d e` | shared across groups | yes | numbered |\n| `#{#{[A B] [B A]} #{[C D] [D C]}}` | `[15]` | `a b c d` | incomplete key | yes | `[:label-bytes]` |\n| `[#{#{X Y}} #{[X Z] [Y W]}]` | `[15 0 :member]` | `w x y z` | shared across groups | yes | numbered |\n| `#{[#{X Y} #{X Y}] [#{P Q} #{R S}]}` | `[15 :member 0]` | `x y` | shared across groups | yes | `[:label-bytes]` |\n| install child holding the 2-cycle | lift `[12 a 15]`; decode of an `emit-numbered` carrier: `:yin.k/undecodable`, path `[<root> 12 a 15]`, cells `[cc-1 cc-2]` | | | | |\n\nGates: two cells under `{:cursor-cells 1}` and 65 cells under the\ndefault budget refuse `[:cursor-cells]`; `[[[[X]]]]` under `{:depth 3}`\nrefuses `[:depth]`; `{:work 1}` refuses `[:work]` at lift and, for a\nvalid carrier, at decode; the 400000-element literal inside a set refuses\n`[:work]`; an unreachable census cell is the defect\n`{:missing [:x], :extra []}`. The 65536-character descriptor state\nnumbers at `:work` 544554 (21290 at 128 characters; encoded comparison\nbytes 132540 against 1718, all charged before encoding).\n\nRandom search (seeded; each state compared with itself renamed and\nshuffled, and decoded through 6a's decoder, whose own retained\nrecomputation budget stops 397 of the carriers at `[:label-bytes]`: a\nprobe limitation, not a failure):\n\n| Generator | Numbered | Refused (interch. / not / undecided) | Only across groups | Count gain | Invariance fail | Decode ok / fail | max `:work` |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| random values, 2000 | 1981 | 19 (16 / 3 / 0) | 7 | 20 | 0 | 1789 / 0 | 624926 |\n| frame-bearing, 200 | 196 | 4 (3 / 1 / 0) | 0 | 4 | 0 | 179 / 0 | 202939 |\n| with children, 100 | 98 | 2 (1 / 0 / 1) | 1 | 0 | 0 | 75 / 0 | 434068 |\n| incidence families, 1000 | 832 | 168 (92 / 17 / 59) | 74 | 295 | 0 | 667 / 0 | 453009 |\n\n\"Only across groups\" counts refusals whose every failing group failed\nonly clause (iii): an upper bound on what the component rule of appendix\nB could recover. \"Count gain\" counts states numbered only because the\nkey carries the occurrence count; the reverse count is 0 everywhere. A\ntest that simply entered ties in host order would have accepted the 21\nstates classified not interchangeable, with host-order-dependent rows:\nthat is why a sound test is needed at all.\n\nSize: the prototype's procedure region (`discovery.clj:79-416`) is 296\nnon-blank lines, of which roughly 80 are docstrings, trace and the two\ndiagnostic switches; 6a's `canonical-numbering` plus\n`check-label-bytes!` is 136 non-blank lines. In `rows.cljc` the\nreplacement reuses `descriptors`, `bytes-key`, `compare-bytes`, `spend!`,\n`refuse!`, the `size` bound and `emit-node`; expect about 200 added or\nreplacement lines and about 135 deleted, excluding tests.\n\n## B. Round 3's variants: components (D4), conflicts (D5) and the residue search (D6). Optional extension, not adopted\n\nRound 3 (`collab/1792220000000-numbering-note.partial-round4.md`, 980\nlines, and `probe/discovery_r3.clj`) resolved surviving ties differently:\nlink tied members that share an unnumbered cell into **components**;\nencode each component by a local run over a synthetic\n`[:component g₁ i₁ g₂ i₂ …]` row; enter the components of least\nencoding; a component holding two members of one group is a **conflict**,\nrefused (D5) or resolved by **trials** (D6): one local run per member of\nthe duplicated group with that member entered first, the least complete\nspelling adopted, charged to `:search`. The base design of §1 keeps none\nof it: no component runs, no trials, no `:search` budget. What the\nextension would add over the base: `[#{X Y} #{X Y}]` and the other\n\"shared across groups\" refusals (at most 82 of 3300 generated states,\nappendix A) via components, and with D6 every state of appendix A's\nrefused table, including the seven-cell state (11 trials) and 6a's own\nroots on the 2-cycle and 3-cycle.\n\n*Proof sketch (round 3, lemmas C and E).* Equal complete component\nencodings are faithful spellings and exhibit a bijection under which the\ncomponents are the same structure group by group; since the cells of\ndistinct components are disjoint and confined, the block swap is an\nautomorphism, so entering a run of equal encodings in host order gives\nthe same rows. For D6, the multiset of trial spellings is canonical if\neach trial is, and equal least spellings give isomorphic numberings. The\nthird review found the induction incomplete: a trial starts from the\nsame numbering and the same cells as its parent, the forced member need\nnot number a cell before recursive work begins, and key runs under D6\nsearch instead of spelling residue; a well-founded measure covering key\nruns, synthetic component runs, forced traversal and recursive trials\nwas never supplied. That, with the reviewer's judgement that D6 is not\nmaterially simpler than 6a, is why it is not adopted.\n\n*Measurements (round 3, `collab/numbering-probe/run-r3.log`).* Under D6:\n2000 random value states, 200 frame-bearing and 100 with children all\nnumbered, 0 invariance failures, 0 decode failures, at most 13 trials\nand 1470 `:work` units (round-3 accounting, positions only); the 2-cycle\nand 3-cycle reached 6a's roots (`0fe898cc…`, `0b181cd4…`), the loop plus\n2-cycle `858a891e…`, the seven-cell state `d76d787a…` in 11 trials and\n488 units; the reviewer's independent 5000-state run found no\ncanonicality counterexample in either mode and 2117 D5 refusals.\n\n## C. Why not portable ids minted in the VM\n\nThe owner asked whether a cursor cell could carry a portable id from\ncreation. Every candidate (branch `srvm-phase6a`,\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc`: the task's id counter `gen-id` `:227-230`\nand `gensym` `:2312-2318`; the creation sequence of `:stream/cursor`\nexecutions, `handle-cursor-unminted` `:557-568`, minted at `:2595-2602`;\na hash of occurrence and counter; a random id) names history, not\nstructure. Two tasks with structurally equal snapshots (two cursor cells\nwith equal bodies at equal positions, equal counters) carry different\nid sets when one created and dropped a third cursor and the other\nconsumed an unrelated fresh name; the cross-engine law would then make\nan execution trace part of the frame's identity, and lower would have to\nkeep a second global name beside its private resource entry. Discovery\norder gives the identity a structural name instead. Recommended against.\n\n## D. History\n\nRound 1 (2026-10-12): discovery order proposed; the orbit lemma was\nunsound (`#{[X X] [Y Z]}`) and nested ties were never processed\n(`#{#{X Y} #{Z W}}`). Round 2: alias-carrying keys, the fixpoint, the\ncomponent rule; the review found speculative key-run conflicts refusing\nwrongly and an install child's refusal escaping decode. Round 3: key\nruns never refuse, the child translation, rank spelling, the `:work`\nbudget, D6; the review found the byte accounting unimplemented, the D6\ninduction incomplete, the coverage paragraph wrong, the `:cursor-cells`\nand `:depth` gates dropped and the large-literal test incompatible with\ntotality. Round 4 (this text): the base design, with every one of those\nfindings addressed in §§1–4 and the variants moved here.\n","numLines":746,"startLine":1,"totalLines":746}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01X1vs7mRGUrzpVsCB8C7Gr4","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
